### Definition: Field Hiding vs Method Overriding

* **Method Overriding (Dynamic)**: When a subclass declares an instance method with the same signature, it overrides the superclass method. Method invocation is **polymorphic** and resolved at runtime based on the **Actual Object Type**.
* **Field Hiding (Static)**: When a subclass declares a variable with the same name as a superclass variable, it **hides** the parent field. Field access is **NOT polymorphic**; it is resolved at **compile time** strictly based on the **Reference Type**.

```text
Vehicle v = new SportsCar();
v.speed;    // Resolves to Vehicle.speed (Reference Type)
v.drive();  // Resolves to SportsCar.drive() (Actual Object Type: Override)
```

---

### Code Demonstration: Field Hiding in Action

```java
class Parent {
    String role = "Parent Role"; // Hidden by child

    void identify() {
        System.out.println("Parent Method: " + role);
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    String role = "Child Role"; // Hiding the parent field!

    @Override
    void identify() {
        System.out.println("Child Method: " + role);
    }
}

public class FieldHidingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Parent p = new Child();

        // 1. Field access is resolved by Reference Type (Parent)
        System.out.println("Direct field access: " + p.role); // Outputs: Parent Role!

        // 2. Method call is resolved by Actual Object Type (Child override)
        p.identify(); // Outputs: Child Method: Child Role!
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
Direct field access: Parent Role
Child Method: Child Role
```

---

### Best Practice: Always Encapsulate Fields

Never declare `public` or package-private fields in inheritance hierarchies. By making fields `private` and exposing `getters`, getters **override** polymorphically, completely eliminating Field Hiding bugs.

